  I am working from the assumption that the machines in question
should have near 24 hour a day availability with no operations
personell on call during "off-hours", such as a mail gateway.  In that
case, you may well need to have swap space available in order to run
with anything that comes close to reasonable performance.  Running for
a short time w/out swap is little better than having the machine down
[both are likely to generate unwanted calls to admins].  Likewise,
it's more difficult for J. Random User to diagnose the problem of not
having swap space available, especially if J. Random doesn't know much
of anything about unix, such as the boss who reads his mail via POP
from a MacIntosh.

  That's what I mean by "invites trouble."  :-)


