In article <CEyGun.Cov@cc.swarthmore.edu>, hirai@cc.swarthmore.edu (Eiji Hirai) writes:
|> If I compile my heavily used programs with "LDFLAGS = -non_shared" (i.e.
|> static-ly link my object files), will this have any speed improvements when
|> I execute the binary?  Is this a useless exercise or will I gain something?

It depends.  Small, frequently executed programs will be faster linked
non-shared.  This is because the startup cost of shared executables is
greater than the cost of nonshared executables.  Another cost
associated with shared libraries is the cost of mapping the entire set
of libraries into your address space when you might only be using a
tiny subset of the code in these libraries.  In some cases reducing
the virtual size of the process is a performance win.  Of course,
having the shared libraries reduces disk space usage, increases
maintainability and allows the library code segments to be shared
at run time.

