>   f:   262144   104542    unused     1024  8192         # (Cyl.  131*- 459*)
>   g:  1684174   366686    unused     1024  8192         # (Cyl.  459*- 2569*)
>   h:   262144   104542    unused     1024  8192         # (Cyl.  131*- 459*)

You should always create disklabels in a such way that partitions that
are not in use, cannot be used. This can be done by setting all values
to zero:

8 partitions:
#        size   offset    fstype   [fsize bsize   cpg]
  a:      798        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - 0)
  b:        0        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - 0)
  c:  2050860        0    unused     1024  8192    16   # (Cyl.    0 - 2569)
  d:  2050062      798    4.2BSD     1024  8192    16   # (Cyl.    1 - 2569)
  e:        0        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - -1)
  f:        0        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - -1)
  g:        0        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - -1)
  h:        0        0    unused        0     0         # (Cyl.    0 - -1)

If you don't have b partition the swapon cannot swap on it. It is also
much easier to guess which partions are in use if you happen to loose
/etc/fstab or something other goes wrong (somebody write 40MB of junk
in the begining of the disk or some other disaster).

> You need to remember that when you change your root devices SCSI address 
> you must change the /sbin/swapdefault for the root device to avoid this 
> situation.

And don't create partitions in disklabel, that you don't use!
