>The lint that comes with OSF/1 v1.3 isn't very good - ie it misses
>things like:
>
>if ( a = b)
>{
>[.....]
>}

hm, this actually surprised me, but I tried it using Ultrix lint and
HP-UX 9.01 lint, and neither one complained about (a=b).  Note that as
the other poster said, the code _will_ get executed unless b=0.

However, gcc -Wall (which is what I usually use, -Wall -pedanic -ansi),
does catch it, saying "Warning: suggest parentheses around assignment
used as truth value", which is nice, and a good habit to get in to.
Borland C (I turn all warnings on there as well), complains "Possibly
incorrect assignment", which is more the heart of it.

hm, and to think I was feeling guilty for never using lint on my code.
But, gcc -Wall -pedantic -ansi is probably just as good, if not better.
Use it.

todd.
